1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,903
In part one, a model learned to guess the

2
00:00:03,903 --> 00:00:07,507
next letter by counting, and it hit a wall.

3
00:00:07,507 --> 00:00:09,909
It could share exact letter patterns,

4
00:00:09,909 --> 00:00:13,112
but each context was an island of counts.

5
00:00:13,112 --> 00:00:16,715
Reading the dog barked a hundred times did not

6
00:00:16,715 --> 00:00:21,819
teach it that dog and hound have related meanings.

7
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:25,563
Every modern model fixes this the same way.

8
00:00:25,563 --> 00:00:30,325
It gives each word a place, a point in a space,

9
00:00:30,325 --> 00:00:34,653
chosen so that words used alike end up close together.

10
00:00:34,653 --> 00:00:38,116
Here are 102 words, where GPT-2 puts them:

11
00:00:38,116 --> 00:00:41,146
animals in one region, colors in another,

12
00:00:41,146 --> 00:00:45,100
then numbers, places, family, the body, and verbs.

13
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,146
The picture is a shadow.

14
00:00:47,146 --> 00:00:52,467
GPT-2 really gives each of its 50,257 tokens a list of 768 numbers,

15
00:00:52,467 --> 00:00:56,559
and we've flattened them down to three, keeping the three

16
00:00:56,559 --> 00:01:00,243
directions along which these words spread out the most.

17
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:05,017
Up close, dog sits with the other animals.

18
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:08,977
Here is dog, all 768 numbers.

19
00:01:08,977 --> 00:01:10,249
Here is puppy.

20
00:01:10,249 --> 00:01:11,945
And here is tobacco.

21
00:01:11,945 --> 00:01:14,064
Two of these belong together,

22
00:01:14,064 --> 00:01:19,804
and in chapter one we'll learn to measure how much.

23
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:22,952
The mathematics is older than the machines.

24
00:01:22,952 --> 00:01:25,396
In 1844 Hermann Grassmann described quantities

25
00:01:25,396 --> 00:01:29,063
that add and stretch, in any number of dimensions.

26
00:01:29,063 --> 00:01:33,137
In 1858 Arthur Cayley wrote down the algebra of matrices.

27
00:01:33,137 --> 00:01:35,989
Beltrami and Jordan found the singular value

28
00:01:35,989 --> 00:01:38,026
decomposition, and Hilbert named eigenvalues.

29
00:01:38,026 --> 00:01:42,100
Eckart and Young proved how to keep only what matters.

30
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:42,915
Then meaning:

31
00:01:42,915 --> 00:01:47,804
John Firth's 'you shall know a word by the company it keeps'.

32
00:01:47,804 --> 00:01:49,841
Johnson and Lindenstrauss squeezed dimensions.

33
00:01:49,841 --> 00:01:53,915
Latent semantic analysis put words in a space, and Netflix

34
00:01:53,915 --> 00:01:58,396
offered a million dollars for filling in a mostly empty matrix.

35
00:01:58,396 --> 00:02:01,248
Then word2vec, and LoRA, GaLore and Muon,

36
00:02:01,248 --> 00:02:05,600
the same linear algebra inside today's training.

37
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:08,988
This is Part 3 of AI, Built.

38
00:02:08,988 --> 00:02:09,870
In it:

39
00:02:09,870 --> 00:02:12,517
vectors, the dot product and einsum;

40
00:02:12,517 --> 00:02:14,723
projections, eigenvectors and the singular

41
00:02:14,723 --> 00:02:16,487
value decomposition, each derived;

42
00:02:16,487 --> 00:02:20,457
norms, random matrices and what training writes into weights;

43
00:02:20,457 --> 00:02:21,339
word vectors;

44
00:02:21,339 --> 00:02:23,545
the geometry of high dimensions;

45
00:02:23,545 --> 00:02:25,751
and low rank at work.

46
00:02:25,751 --> 00:02:29,280
Every number computed, small enough for a laptop.

47
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:32,124
Chapter one.

48
00:02:32,124 --> 00:02:37,167
Vectors, and how alike two of them are.

49
00:02:37,167 --> 00:02:40,469
A vector is an arrow from the origin,

50
00:02:40,469 --> 00:02:43,771
or, just as well, a list of numbers:

51
00:02:43,771 --> 00:02:46,660
how far to go along each axis.

52
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:52,851
Two numbers in the plane, three in space, and 768 for a word in GPT-2.

53
00:02:52,851 --> 00:02:55,740
Grassmann saw in 1844 that the algebra

54
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:58,629
doesn't care how many numbers there are.

55
00:02:58,629 --> 00:03:03,019
Almost nobody read him for forty years.

56
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,255
Two moves make all of linear algebra.

57
00:03:06,255 --> 00:03:07,521
Add two vectors:

58
00:03:07,521 --> 00:03:10,054
walk along one, then the other.

59
00:03:10,054 --> 00:03:11,320
And scale them:

60
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,008
stretch, shrink or flip.

61
00:03:13,008 --> 00:03:17,229
Mix the two, a times u plus b times v,

62
00:03:17,229 --> 00:03:20,606
and the tip sweeps out the whole plane:

63
00:03:20,606 --> 00:03:24,827
u and v are a basis, and every vector has

64
00:03:24,827 --> 00:03:29,281
exactly one pair of coordinates in it.

65
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:31,765
How alike are two vectors?

66
00:03:31,765 --> 00:03:34,657
Multiply them coordinate by coordinate and add:

67
00:03:34,657 --> 00:03:37,135
the dot product, or inner product.

68
00:03:37,135 --> 00:03:40,026
It is symmetric, linear in each slot,

69
00:03:40,026 --> 00:03:44,157
and a vector dotted with itself is its length squared.

70
00:03:44,157 --> 00:03:48,700
Draw the triangle with sides a, b and a minus b.

71
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:52,005
Its third side squared is a dot product.

72
00:03:52,005 --> 00:03:52,831
Expand it.

73
00:03:52,831 --> 00:03:56,962
Euclid's law of cosines gives the same side another way.

74
00:03:56,962 --> 00:03:57,375
Compare:

75
00:03:57,375 --> 00:04:00,679
the dot product is the two lengths times

76
00:04:00,679 --> 00:04:05,070
the cosine of the angle between them.

77
00:04:05,367 --> 00:04:10,806
Is that cosine always between minus one and one, even in 768 dimensions?

78
00:04:10,806 --> 00:04:12,899
That's the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.

79
00:04:12,899 --> 00:04:19,175
For any number t, the length of a minus t b, squared, can't be negative.

80
00:04:19,175 --> 00:04:22,523
If b is zero, the inequality is immediate.

81
00:04:22,523 --> 00:04:23,778
Otherwise, expand it:

82
00:04:23,778 --> 00:04:25,452
a parabola in t.

83
00:04:25,452 --> 00:04:30,474
A parabola that never dips below zero has at most one root,

84
00:04:30,474 --> 00:04:32,984
so its discriminant can't be positive.

85
00:04:32,984 --> 00:04:33,403
Rearranged:

86
00:04:33,403 --> 00:04:38,424
the absolute dot product is at most the product of the lengths.

87
00:04:38,424 --> 00:04:40,098
Turn b and watch:

88
00:04:40,098 --> 00:04:45,538
the parabola touches zero when b lies on a's line, in either direction.

89
00:04:45,538 --> 00:04:50,385
So an angle between two words makes sense.

90
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:55,064
Divide by the two lengths and you get the cosine

91
00:04:55,064 --> 00:04:58,609
similarity, which runs from minus one to one.

92
00:04:58,609 --> 00:04:59,938
For GPT-2's words:

93
00:04:59,938 --> 00:05:01,711
dog and puppy, 0.62.

94
00:05:01,711 --> 00:05:03,483
Dog and cat, 0.55.

95
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:05,256
Dog and tobacco, 0.23.

96
00:05:05,256 --> 00:05:11,016
In 768 dimensions you can't draw the angle, but you can compute it:

97
00:05:11,016 --> 00:05:16,504
multiply the two lists number by number, and add.

98
00:05:16,767 --> 00:05:18,852
Here is why this matters.

99
00:05:18,852 --> 00:05:24,690
In Part 1, a network ended with a score for every possible next token,

100
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:27,610
and the softmax turned scores into probabilities.

101
00:05:27,610 --> 00:05:30,946
In GPT-2, each score is a dot product:

102
00:05:30,946 --> 00:05:34,282
the model's final state with each token's vector,

103
00:05:34,282 --> 00:05:37,619
the very vectors the tokens started out as.

104
00:05:37,619 --> 00:05:41,789
After the Eiffel Tower is located in the city of,

105
00:05:41,789 --> 00:05:45,542
the biggest dot products belong to Paris, London, Amsterdam.

106
00:05:45,542 --> 00:05:48,044
The softmax gives Paris 6.4 percent.

107
00:05:50,030 --> 00:05:50,921
Chapter two.

108
00:05:50,921 --> 00:05:55,964
Tensors, and the notation attention is written in.

109
00:05:55,964 --> 00:05:58,528
A number, a vector, a matrix:

110
00:05:58,528 --> 00:06:00,665
zero, one and two axes.

111
00:06:00,665 --> 00:06:04,511
A batch of text is a list of sentences,

112
00:06:04,511 --> 00:06:08,785
each a list of tokens, each a list of numbers:

113
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:12,204
three axes, what machine learning calls a tensor.

114
00:06:12,204 --> 00:06:15,196
Attention splits the numbers among 12 heads:

115
00:06:15,196 --> 00:06:15,624
four.

116
00:06:15,624 --> 00:06:21,825
Products of such arrays need a notation that names the axes.

117
00:06:22,097 --> 00:06:23,324
Multiplying two matrices:

118
00:06:23,324 --> 00:06:29,869
entry i k is row i of A against column k of B, summed over j.

119
00:06:29,869 --> 00:06:31,915
In 1916, writing general relativity,

120
00:06:31,915 --> 00:06:35,187
Einstein tired of summation signs and dropped them:

121
00:06:35,187 --> 00:06:38,050
an index that appears twice is summed.

122
00:06:38,050 --> 00:06:42,959
Programs adopted the idea as einsum, with a letter for each axis.

123
00:06:42,959 --> 00:06:45,823
A letter in the output is kept;

124
00:06:45,823 --> 00:06:49,504
a letter only in the inputs is summed away.

125
00:06:49,504 --> 00:06:54,413
And the cost is one multiply-add for every combination of the letters:

126
00:06:54,413 --> 00:06:57,958
i times j times k.

127
00:06:58,230 --> 00:07:00,877
One function does all of these.

128
00:07:00,877 --> 00:07:02,200
The dot product:

129
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:03,964
multiply, and sum everything.

130
00:07:03,964 --> 00:07:05,287
The outer product:

131
00:07:05,287 --> 00:07:09,256
nothing summed, a matrix of rank at most one.

132
00:07:09,256 --> 00:07:10,579
Matrix times vector.

133
00:07:10,579 --> 00:07:12,784
The transpose, which only reorders.

134
00:07:12,784 --> 00:07:13,666
The trace.

135
00:07:13,666 --> 00:07:20,017
And a batch of matrix products, the letter b carried along.

136
00:07:20,297 --> 00:07:24,122
Here is attention, Part 13's subject, in this notation.

137
00:07:24,122 --> 00:07:27,097
Each token's vector is projected into queries,

138
00:07:27,097 --> 00:07:29,646
keys and values, split among heads.

139
00:07:29,646 --> 00:07:32,621
Every query is dotted with every key:

140
00:07:32,621 --> 00:07:34,321
e is summed away.

141
00:07:34,321 --> 00:07:38,571
A softmax turns scores into weights, which average the values.

142
00:07:38,571 --> 00:07:40,696
The heads are merged back.

143
00:07:40,696 --> 00:07:43,245
Read the costs off the letters.

144
00:07:43,245 --> 00:07:47,070
For 1,024 tokens, the scores take about 805 million

145
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:50,470
multiply-adds per layer, and the projections 2,416 million.

146
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:54,295
The scores grow with the square of the length:

147
00:07:54,295 --> 00:07:58,769
that is why long contexts are expensive.

148
00:07:59,030 --> 00:08:02,188
The letters also show that order matters.

149
00:08:02,188 --> 00:08:08,955
A is 1,000 by 20, B is 20 by 1,000, C is 1,000 by 20.

150
00:08:08,955 --> 00:08:12,565
Multiply A and B first, and you build

151
00:08:12,565 --> 00:08:16,174
a 1,000 by 1,000 matrix on the way:

152
00:08:16,174 --> 00:08:17,076
40,000,000 multiply-adds.

153
00:08:17,076 --> 00:08:18,881
B and C first:

154
00:08:18,881 --> 00:08:19,332
800,000.

155
00:08:19,332 --> 00:08:22,039
The same answer, 50 times cheaper.

156
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:25,196
einsum can find the cheap order itself;

157
00:08:25,196 --> 00:08:28,354
here it multiplies B and C first.

158
00:08:30,095 --> 00:08:31,036
Chapter three.

159
00:08:31,036 --> 00:08:33,947
Matrices move space.

160
00:08:33,961 --> 00:08:36,937
A matrix moves every vector at once.

161
00:08:36,937 --> 00:08:39,912
It keeps the origin where it is,

162
00:08:39,912 --> 00:08:42,038
and keeps grid lines straight,

163
00:08:42,038 --> 00:08:43,738
parallel, and evenly spaced:

164
00:08:43,738 --> 00:08:45,438
it's a linear map.

165
00:08:45,438 --> 00:08:49,063
Watch the whole plane move.

166
00:08:49,561 --> 00:08:51,578
Where does each vector go?

167
00:08:51,578 --> 00:08:53,595
Only two arrows decide it:

168
00:08:53,595 --> 00:08:56,016
where the two basis arrows land.

169
00:08:56,016 --> 00:08:59,646
Those landing spots are the columns of the matrix.

170
00:08:59,646 --> 00:09:03,680
A vector with coordinates one and two lands at one

171
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:07,311
times the first column, plus two times the second.

172
00:09:07,311 --> 00:09:12,441
That's what multiplying a matrix by a vector means.

173
00:09:12,695 --> 00:09:16,021
One map after another is again a map,

174
00:09:16,021 --> 00:09:19,764
and its matrix is the product of the two.

175
00:09:19,764 --> 00:09:21,427
But the order matters:

176
00:09:21,427 --> 00:09:24,754
turning, then shearing, is not shearing, then turning.

177
00:09:24,754 --> 00:09:27,664
Arthur Cayley made matrices objects in their

178
00:09:27,664 --> 00:09:30,991
own right in 1858, with exactly this rule;

179
00:09:30,991 --> 00:09:37,065
his friend James Joseph Sylvester had named them eight years before.

180
00:09:37,361 --> 00:09:41,101
One number says how much a map stretches area:

181
00:09:41,101 --> 00:09:41,931
the determinant.

182
00:09:41,931 --> 00:09:44,424
The unit square becomes a parallelogram:

183
00:09:44,424 --> 00:09:48,994
its area is the absolute determinant, and the sign records orientation.

184
00:09:48,994 --> 00:09:51,071
When the determinant reaches zero,

185
00:09:51,071 --> 00:09:54,395
the whole plane is squashed onto a line,

186
00:09:54,395 --> 00:09:56,888
and information is lost for good.

187
00:09:56,888 --> 00:10:00,211
Below zero, the plane has been flipped over,

188
00:10:00,211 --> 00:10:04,204
like a page turned face down.

189
00:10:04,495 --> 00:10:10,562
That squashing has a name, and it will matter for the rest of this part.

190
00:10:10,562 --> 00:10:14,202
This matrix sends the whole plane onto one line:

191
00:10:14,202 --> 00:10:16,629
its columns point the same way.

192
00:10:16,629 --> 00:10:20,269
The line where everything lands is the column space,

193
00:10:20,269 --> 00:10:23,504
and its dimension is the rank, here one.

194
00:10:23,504 --> 00:10:26,740
A whole line of vectors lands on zero:

195
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:27,954
the null space.

196
00:10:27,954 --> 00:10:29,976
The dimensions always add up:

197
00:10:29,976 --> 00:10:34,425
rank plus the null space's dimension is the number of columns.

198
00:10:34,425 --> 00:10:38,065
A matrix of low rank is a big table

199
00:10:38,065 --> 00:10:40,087
that carries few independent directions.

200
00:10:41,828 --> 00:10:42,783
Chapter four.

201
00:10:42,783 --> 00:10:46,670
Projections, and the closest point.

202
00:10:46,694 --> 00:10:49,221
The dot product measured a shadow;

203
00:10:49,221 --> 00:10:51,747
here is the shadow made exact.

204
00:10:51,747 --> 00:10:56,379
Which point on the line through a is closest to b?

205
00:10:56,379 --> 00:10:59,747
Some multiple of a, the one whose error,

206
00:10:59,747 --> 00:11:03,536
b minus that point, is perpendicular to the line:

207
00:11:03,536 --> 00:11:06,484
any other point is farther, by Pythagoras.

208
00:11:06,484 --> 00:11:08,168
Solve for the multiple.

209
00:11:08,168 --> 00:11:11,115
The projection is a matrix times b:

210
00:11:11,115 --> 00:11:14,483
a times a transpose, over a dot a.

211
00:11:14,483 --> 00:11:19,352
A matrix of rank one, that finds shadows.

212
00:11:19,628 --> 00:11:21,706
Now project onto a subspace:

213
00:11:21,706 --> 00:11:26,693
everything you can reach by combining the columns of a matrix A.

214
00:11:26,693 --> 00:11:30,433
The closest point is A times some x hat,

215
00:11:30,433 --> 00:11:34,589
and the error is perpendicular to every column at once.

216
00:11:34,589 --> 00:11:35,420
Multiply out:

217
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:36,667
the normal equations.

218
00:11:36,667 --> 00:11:41,238
If the columns are independent, A transpose A can be inverted.

219
00:11:41,238 --> 00:11:42,485
The projection matrix:

220
00:11:42,485 --> 00:11:45,394
A, A transpose A inverse, A transpose.

221
00:11:45,394 --> 00:11:47,887
Project twice and nothing more happens;

222
00:11:47,887 --> 00:11:49,550
and P is symmetric.

223
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:52,874
Legendre published this in 1805 as least squares,

224
00:11:52,874 --> 00:12:00,193
and Gauss in 1809, who had used it to find the lost asteroid Ceres.

225
00:12:00,428 --> 00:12:03,684
This is the most used calculation in statistics.

226
00:12:03,684 --> 00:12:05,719
Fourteen points and a line.

227
00:12:05,719 --> 00:12:09,790
The columns of A are ones and the x values;

228
00:12:09,790 --> 00:12:15,081
the best line is the projection of the y values onto their span.

229
00:12:15,081 --> 00:12:17,930
The errors are perpendicular to both columns:

230
00:12:17,930 --> 00:12:22,408
they sum to zero, and so do the errors times x.

231
00:12:22,408 --> 00:12:26,885
Regression, a linear probe reading a concept out of a network,

232
00:12:26,885 --> 00:12:29,734
a last layer trained with squared error:

233
00:12:29,734 --> 00:12:32,456
all this projection.

234
00:12:32,694 --> 00:12:35,596
Projections are easiest with an orthonormal basis,

235
00:12:35,596 --> 00:12:37,668
perpendicular arrows of length one:

236
00:12:37,668 --> 00:12:40,984
then the projection is Q times Q transpose.

237
00:12:40,984 --> 00:12:44,715
Gram and Schmidt's recipe makes one from any basis.

238
00:12:44,715 --> 00:12:47,616
Scale the first vector to length one.

239
00:12:47,616 --> 00:12:51,346
From the second, remove its shadow on the first;

240
00:12:51,346 --> 00:12:53,419
what is left is perpendicular.

241
00:12:53,419 --> 00:12:55,077
Scale it, and repeat.

242
00:12:55,077 --> 00:12:59,636
Keeping the coefficients writes A as Q times a triangular R:

243
00:12:59,636 --> 00:13:02,380
the QR factorization.

244
00:13:02,628 --> 00:13:07,100
On a computer every step rounds, and rounding can pile up.

245
00:13:07,100 --> 00:13:11,166
We tested on Hilbert matrices, whose columns become nearly dependent.

246
00:13:11,166 --> 00:13:13,199
The classical recipe loses orthogonality

247
00:13:13,199 --> 00:13:16,045
as the square of the condition number:

248
00:13:16,045 --> 00:13:19,704
at a condition number of ten to the 6,

249
00:13:19,704 --> 00:13:24,176
its Q is off by about ten to the minus 6.

250
00:13:24,176 --> 00:13:28,242
By the end, its vectors are not perpendicular at all.

251
00:13:28,242 --> 00:13:31,495
The modified recipe, which takes each shadow from

252
00:13:31,495 --> 00:13:34,341
the updated vector, loses only in proportion.

253
00:13:34,341 --> 00:13:37,187
Alston Householder's reflections, from 1958, stay perpendicular

254
00:13:37,187 --> 00:13:41,253
to about ten to the minus 15, the machine's precision.

255
00:13:41,253 --> 00:13:44,099
The normal equations share the classical flaw:

256
00:13:44,099 --> 00:13:47,351
forming A transpose A squares the condition number.

257
00:13:47,351 --> 00:13:53,043
At the hardest case QR's answer is off by ten to the minus 1,

258
00:13:53,043 --> 00:13:57,922
against an error bigger than the answer itself for the normal equations.

259
00:13:57,922 --> 00:14:01,581
That is why libraries solve least squares with QR.

260
00:14:03,361 --> 00:14:04,316
Chapter five.

261
00:14:04,316 --> 00:14:08,203
Eigenvectors, and the spectral theorem.

262
00:14:08,227 --> 00:14:12,412
Most vectors are knocked off their line by a matrix.

263
00:14:12,412 --> 00:14:13,249
Some aren't:

264
00:14:13,249 --> 00:14:17,433
they stay on their line, only stretched, shrunk or flipped.

265
00:14:17,433 --> 00:14:21,618
Those are the eigenvectors, and the scale is the eigenvalue.

266
00:14:21,618 --> 00:14:25,384
A minus lambda I must then squash a direction

267
00:14:25,384 --> 00:14:27,894
to zero, so its determinant vanishes:

268
00:14:27,894 --> 00:14:31,487
a polynomial equation for lambda.

269
00:14:31,761 --> 00:14:34,352
Symmetric matrices, equal to their mirror

270
00:14:34,352 --> 00:14:36,944
image across the diagonal, behave best.

271
00:14:36,944 --> 00:14:39,967
Their eigenvalues are real, and we can

272
00:14:39,967 --> 00:14:42,127
choose an orthonormal eigenvector basis.

273
00:14:42,127 --> 00:14:43,854
So the map is:

274
00:14:43,854 --> 00:14:46,446
turn the eigenvectors onto the axes,

275
00:14:46,446 --> 00:14:50,333
stretch each axis by its eigenvalue, and turn back.

276
00:14:50,333 --> 00:14:52,924
A equals Q, Lambda, Q transpose;

277
00:14:52,924 --> 00:14:58,107
for a turn like Q, the transpose is the same turn, undone.

278
00:14:58,107 --> 00:15:01,335
That's the spectral theorem.

279
00:15:01,627 --> 00:15:03,719
Why does it always work?

280
00:15:03,719 --> 00:15:05,811
The proof has three steps.

281
00:15:05,811 --> 00:15:07,903
First, the eigenvalues are real:

282
00:15:07,903 --> 00:15:11,668
sandwich A between an eigenvector and its complex conjugate.

283
00:15:11,668 --> 00:15:15,433
Since A is real and symmetric, the middle number

284
00:15:15,433 --> 00:15:19,198
equals its own conjugate, so lambda equals lambda bar.

285
00:15:19,198 --> 00:15:22,127
Second, eigenvectors with different eigenvalues are perpendicular:

286
00:15:22,127 --> 00:15:25,892
move A across the dot product, and the eigenvalues

287
00:15:25,892 --> 00:15:29,239
must agree unless the dot product is zero.

288
00:15:29,239 --> 00:15:32,585
Third, A maps the space perpendicular to an

289
00:15:32,585 --> 00:15:35,514
eigenvector into itself, so repeat inside it:

290
00:15:35,514 --> 00:15:37,606
by induction, a full set.

291
00:15:37,606 --> 00:15:40,534
Cauchy proved the first part in 1829;

292
00:15:40,534 --> 00:15:44,544
Hilbert took it to infinite dimensions.

293
00:15:44,827 --> 00:15:47,695
One number connects eigenvalues to what follows:

294
00:15:47,695 --> 00:15:52,201
the Rayleigh quotient, x transpose A x over x transpose x.

295
00:15:52,201 --> 00:15:57,117
In the eigenvector basis it is a weighted average of the eigenvalues.

296
00:15:57,117 --> 00:16:01,623
So as x turns, it stays between the smallest and the

297
00:16:01,623 --> 00:16:04,491
largest eigenvalue, reaching them along the eigenvectors.

298
00:16:04,491 --> 00:16:06,949
The top eigenvector answers a question:

299
00:16:06,949 --> 00:16:09,407
which direction does A favour most?

300
00:16:09,407 --> 00:16:15,003
It returns as principal components, power iteration and the SVD.

301
00:16:15,294 --> 00:16:17,737
Covariance matrices describe how data spreads:

302
00:16:17,737 --> 00:16:20,994
how much each coordinate varies, on the diagonal,

303
00:16:20,994 --> 00:16:24,251
and how much pairs vary together, off it.

304
00:16:24,251 --> 00:16:28,730
The spread along a unit direction is the covariance's Rayleigh quotient,

305
00:16:28,730 --> 00:16:32,801
so the direction of greatest spread is the top eigenvector.

306
00:16:32,801 --> 00:16:34,837
These are the principal components,

307
00:16:34,837 --> 00:16:37,687
Pearson's in 1901 and Hotelling's in 1933.

308
00:16:37,687 --> 00:16:42,979
Keeping the top few is how we drew GPT-2's words in three dimensions.

309
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:45,651
Chapter six.

310
00:16:45,651 --> 00:16:46,542
Power iteration:

311
00:16:46,542 --> 00:16:50,693
how a computer finds an eigenvector.

312
00:16:50,693 --> 00:16:53,173
Eigenvalues are roots of a polynomial,

313
00:16:53,173 --> 00:16:56,892
of degree 768 for a 768 by 768 matrix.

314
00:16:56,892 --> 00:17:00,611
Abel proved there is no general formula using radicals

315
00:17:00,611 --> 00:17:03,917
beyond degree four, so computers use numerical methods.

316
00:17:03,917 --> 00:17:07,223
For this symmetric matrix, start with a nonzero

317
00:17:07,223 --> 00:17:09,289
component along its dominant eigenvector,

318
00:17:09,289 --> 00:17:12,595
multiply by A, rescale to length one, repeat.

319
00:17:12,595 --> 00:17:16,314
Here, each multiplication favours the direction A stretches most,

320
00:17:16,314 --> 00:17:21,119
and the arrow swings onto the top eigenvector.

321
00:17:21,493 --> 00:17:23,909
Why it works takes four lines.

322
00:17:23,909 --> 00:17:27,532
Take a symmetric matrix and a start with a

323
00:17:27,532 --> 00:17:29,947
nonzero component along its dominant eigenvector.

324
00:17:29,947 --> 00:17:33,167
Write the start in the orthonormal eigenvector basis.

325
00:17:33,167 --> 00:17:37,595
k multiplications stretch each part by its eigenvalue to the k.

326
00:17:37,595 --> 00:17:39,206
Pull out the largest:

327
00:17:39,206 --> 00:17:44,439
every other part is multiplied by a ratio below one, to the k.

328
00:17:44,439 --> 00:17:49,672
So the angle shrinks like lambda two over lambda one, to the k:

329
00:17:49,672 --> 00:17:52,490
a straight line on a log scale.

330
00:17:52,490 --> 00:17:55,308
The eigenvalue estimate does twice as well,

331
00:17:55,308 --> 00:17:58,930
because the Rayleigh quotient sits at a stationary point,

332
00:17:58,930 --> 00:18:03,651
where small errors in direction barely move it.

333
00:18:03,893 --> 00:18:05,232
Now real matrices.

334
00:18:05,232 --> 00:18:09,692
The covariance of GPT-2's 50,257 token vectors, 768 by 768:

335
