344
00:18:39,560 --> 00:18:42,823
The method is from 1929, by Richard von

345
00:18:42,823 --> 00:18:44,455
Mises and Hilda Pollaczek-Geiringer.

346
00:18:44,455 --> 00:18:46,902
In 1998 it ranked the web:

347
00:18:46,902 --> 00:18:50,981
PageRank is the top eigenvector of a matrix of links.

348
00:18:50,981 --> 00:18:54,651
In 2018, spectral normalization ran one power step per

349
00:18:54,651 --> 00:18:58,322
training step to keep layers from stretching too much.

350
00:18:58,322 --> 00:19:01,177
And today's library routines are its refinement:

351
00:19:01,177 --> 00:19:04,032
Krylov methods, after Cornelius Lanczos in 1950.

352
00:19:05,826 --> 00:19:06,734
Chapter seven.

353
00:19:06,734 --> 00:19:10,026
The singular value decomposition.

354
00:19:10,026 --> 00:19:12,191
Not every matrix is symmetric.

355
00:19:12,191 --> 00:19:13,922
Many aren't even square.

356
00:19:13,922 --> 00:19:17,385
But every matrix, without exception, has three factors:

357
00:19:17,385 --> 00:19:19,550
an orthogonal change of coordinates,

358
00:19:19,550 --> 00:19:22,580
a stretch or projection along the axes,

359
00:19:22,580 --> 00:19:24,311
and another orthogonal change.

360
00:19:24,311 --> 00:19:27,341
Those coordinate changes may rotate or reflect.

361
00:19:27,341 --> 00:19:29,506
Our example uses two rotations.

362
00:19:29,506 --> 00:19:31,670
That's the singular value decomposition:

363
00:19:31,670 --> 00:19:35,767
A equals U, Sigma, V transpose.

364
00:19:36,026 --> 00:19:39,294
Where do the turns and stretches come from?

365
00:19:39,294 --> 00:19:42,562
A transpose A is symmetric, and never negative:

366
00:19:42,562 --> 00:19:46,647
x transpose A transpose A x is a length squared.

367
00:19:46,647 --> 00:19:49,506
So the spectral theorem gives it perpendicular

368
00:19:49,506 --> 00:19:51,957
eigenvectors v, with eigenvalues sigma squared.

369
00:19:51,957 --> 00:19:56,858
Send each v through A and divide by sigma, for nonzero sigma.

370
00:19:56,858 --> 00:20:00,534
Complete the orthonormal bases for any zero singular values.

371
00:20:00,534 --> 00:20:04,211
These u's are perpendicular too, and of length one.

372
00:20:04,211 --> 00:20:06,661
So A sends perpendicular v's to

373
00:20:06,661 --> 00:20:09,112
perpendicular u's, stretched by the sigmas:

374
00:20:09,112 --> 00:20:09,929
the decomposition.

375
00:20:09,929 --> 00:20:13,605
Beltrami and Jordan found it in 1873 and 1874;

376
00:20:13,605 --> 00:20:16,056
Gene Golub and William Kahan's algorithm

377
00:20:16,056 --> 00:20:20,415
of 1965 is what computers still use.

378
00:20:20,693 --> 00:20:23,130
The stretches are the singular values.

379
00:20:23,130 --> 00:20:25,972
The unit circle always becomes an ellipse,

380
00:20:25,972 --> 00:20:28,815
and the singular values are its half-axes.

381
00:20:28,815 --> 00:20:34,906
Sweep a unit vector all the way around, and watch how long its image is.

382
00:20:34,906 --> 00:20:38,155
The longest it ever gets is sigma one:

383
00:20:38,155 --> 00:20:41,403
the most the matrix can stretch any vector.

384
00:20:41,403 --> 00:20:43,434
It's called the spectral norm.

385
00:20:45,226 --> 00:20:46,078
Chapter eight.

386
00:20:46,078 --> 00:20:50,560
The best low-rank copy of a matrix.

387
00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:55,496
Written out, the decomposition makes any matrix a sum of simple pieces,

388
00:20:55,496 --> 00:21:00,021
each a column times a row, weighted by a singular value.

389
00:21:00,021 --> 00:21:02,900
The number of pieces is the rank;

390
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:06,603
a low-rank table is one a few pieces build.

391
00:21:06,603 --> 00:21:11,950
Keep the k biggest and you get the closest matrix of rank k.

392
00:21:11,950 --> 00:21:13,596
Nothing else comes closer:

393
00:21:13,596 --> 00:21:17,152
the Eckart-Young theorem, from 1936.

394
00:21:17,426 --> 00:21:19,481
Why can't anything beat them?

395
00:21:19,481 --> 00:21:21,125
Weyl's inequality, from 1912:

396
00:21:21,125 --> 00:21:23,590
A's singular values, shifted by k,

397
00:21:23,590 --> 00:21:29,754
are at most those of A minus B plus the k plus first of B.

398
00:21:29,754 --> 00:21:33,863
If B has rank k, that last term is zero.

399
00:21:33,863 --> 00:21:38,794
A matrix's squared size equals the sum of its squared singular values,

400
00:21:38,794 --> 00:21:44,547
as chapter nine shows, so B's error is at least the tail of A's.

401
00:21:44,547 --> 00:21:49,334
Keeping the top k achieves exactly that tail.

402
00:21:49,626 --> 00:21:52,115
A picture is a matrix too:

403
00:21:52,115 --> 00:21:54,603
180 rows of 288 grey levels.

404
00:21:54,603 --> 00:21:58,749
Its best rank-one copy is a column times a row:

405
00:21:58,749 --> 00:22:01,237
stripes, the sky and the horizon.

406
00:22:01,237 --> 00:22:02,896
Rank two, four, eight:

407
00:22:02,896 --> 00:22:04,140
the mountains appear.

408
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:07,457
Rank 8 keeps 99.6 percent of the squared

409
00:22:07,457 --> 00:22:10,360
size with 7 percent of the numbers.

410
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:11,604
Then the trees;

411
00:22:11,604 --> 00:22:16,166
by rank 64 it is hard to tell from the original.

412
00:22:16,166 --> 00:22:19,068
And each Frobenius error is exactly the

413
00:22:19,068 --> 00:22:23,886
Euclidean norm of the singular values left out.

414
00:22:24,160 --> 00:22:26,770
