But 62 lie strictly beyond the plotted edge, with no two-percent margin;

514
00:28:01,996 --> 00:28:04,947
the largest is 8.7 times the edge.

515
00:28:04,947 --> 00:28:09,162
This is a finite-sample comparison, not a hard support guarantee.

516
00:28:09,162 --> 00:28:10,426
Just unusual values?

517
00:28:10,426 --> 00:28:13,377
Shuffle the same numbers into random places.

518
00:28:13,377 --> 00:28:15,484
After shuffling, 0 eigenvalues lie

519
00:28:15,484 --> 00:28:18,013
strictly beyond the same limiting edge.

520
00:28:18,013 --> 00:28:20,542
The information is in the arrangement:

521
00:28:20,542 --> 00:28:24,150
a few strong directions, learned.

522
00:28:24,424 --> 00:28:28,925
We can summarize a spectral tail with a fitted power-law density,

523
00:28:28,925 --> 00:28:31,790
falling as lambda to the minus alpha.

524
00:28:31,790 --> 00:28:35,882
In 2019 Charles Martin and Michael Mahoney used random matrix

525
00:28:35,882 --> 00:28:39,155
theory to study heavy-tailed structure in trained networks.

526
00:28:39,155 --> 00:28:44,066
Here the shaded band from 1.5 to 5 is only a descriptive

527
00:28:44,066 --> 00:28:46,521
comparison, not a universal accuracy criterion.

528
00:28:46,521 --> 00:28:50,613
Of GPT-2's 48 matrices, 46 fits fall in that band.

529
00:28:50,613 --> 00:28:53,068
Our displayed layer has alpha 3.53,

530
00:28:53,068 --> 00:28:56,751
from 102 tail eigenvalues, with two-sided KS distance 0.036.

531
00:28:56,751 --> 00:29:00,843
A fitted exponent alone does not establish a power-law model.

532
00:29:00,843 --> 00:29:03,708
The independent Gaussian reference has a compact

533
00:29:03,708 --> 00:29:08,072
limiting bulk, rather than a power-law tail.

534
00:29:08,357 --> 00:29:11,575
The same in this series' small novels model.

535
00:29:11,575 --> 00:29:14,390
At initialization its 30 matrices are random:

536
00:29:14,390 --> 00:29:18,010
7 eigenvalues strictly above their limiting edges in all.

537
00:29:18,010 --> 00:29:22,434
After training, every matrix has between 13 and 32 strictly beyond

538
00:29:22,434 --> 00:29:26,456
the edge, using the same threshold with no two-percent margin.

539
00:29:26,456 --> 00:29:30,478
Training writes a little structure into a sea of noise.

540
00:29:32,224 --> 00:29:33,131
Chapter eleven.

541
00:29:33,131 --> 00:29:36,424
Gradients are low rank.

542
00:29:36,424 --> 00:29:40,914
A gradient is a matrix too, with its own singular values.

543
00:29:40,914 --> 00:29:44,995
A layer turns each token's vector x into W x.

544
00:29:44,995 --> 00:29:50,301
By the chain rule, Part 4's subject, the gradient for one weight sums,

545
00:29:50,301 --> 00:29:54,383
over tokens, the error at that output times the input.

546
00:29:54,383 --> 00:29:58,056
So the gradient is a sum of outer products,

547
00:29:58,056 --> 00:30:01,322
each of rank at most one per token.

548
00:30:01,322 --> 00:30:04,995
Its rank is at most the number of tokens.

549
00:30:04,995 --> 00:30:08,260
In practice it is often approximately low rank:

550
00:30:08,260 --> 00:30:13,026
errors and inputs concentrate in a few directions.

551
00:30:13,291 --> 00:30:18,865
Here is a real gradient, from a small transformer trained on the novels:

552
00:30:18,865 --> 00:30:21,009
one 336 by 128 matrix.

553
00:30:21,009 --> 00:30:24,868
Its singular values, largest first, on a log scale.

554
00:30:24,868 --> 00:30:30,443
At step 1, the top eight carry 54.0 percent of squared gradient energy.

555
00:30:30,443 --> 00:30:34,302
The largest measured top-eight share is at step 50:

556
00:30:34,302 --> 00:30:35,160
100.0 percent.

557
00:30:35,160 --> 00:30:40,735
At the final step, 600, it is 79.0 percent, of 128 possible directions.

558
00:30:40,735 --> 00:30:43,736
The cursor aligns each spectrum with its

559
00:30:43,736 --> 00:30:47,809
position on the full energy-share trajectory.

560
00:30:48,091 --> 00:30:51,043
That is GaLore's idea, from Jiawei Zhao,

561
00:30:51,043 --> 00:30:54,418
Anima Anandkumar, Yuandong Tian and colleagues in 2024.

562
00:30:54,418 --> 00:30:57,371
Adam keeps two running averages per weight:

563
00:30:57,371 --> 00:31:00,746
fourteen billion extra numbers for a seven-billion-parameter model.

564
00:31:00,746 --> 00:31:03,699
GaLore projects each gradient onto its top

565
00:31:03,699 --> 00:31:06,652
r singular directions and runs Adam there,

566
00:31:06,652 --> 00:31:09,183
refreshing the directions now and then.

567
00:31:09,183 --> 00:31:10,448
For our model:

568
00:31:10,448 --> 00:31:15,510
778,240 numbers with Adam, 235,520 at rank 32, 58,880 at rank 8.

569
00:31:15,510 --> 00:31:20,572
At scale the paper reports up to 65 percent less optimizer memory,

570
00:31:20,572 --> 00:31:22,682
and demonstrates that seven-billion-parameter pretraining

571
00:31:22,682 --> 00:31:26,056
can fit within 24 gigabytes, with 8-bit states,

572
00:31:26,056 --> 00:31:29,009
per-layer updates, and a small token batch.

573
00:31:29,009 --> 00:31:32,618
This is a memory-feasibility result.

574
00:31:32,891 --> 00:31:35,389
Does a narrow window cost anything?

575
00:31:35,389 --> 00:31:41,218
We trained the model with Adam and with GaLore at ranks 32 and 8,

576
00:31:41,218 --> 00:31:44,549
each at its best of three learning rates

577
00:31:44,549 --> 00:31:47,047
chosen using seed zero's validation result.

578
00:31:47,047 --> 00:31:51,627
We then run that rate on seeds zero, one and two;

579
00:31:51,627 --> 00:31:54,542
the reported three include the tuning seed.

580
00:31:54,542 --> 00:31:58,289
Training curves show the mean and minimum-to-maximum band after

581
00:31:58,289 --> 00:32:02,036
an eight-sample moving average, with no hard loss clipping.

582
00:32:02,036 --> 00:32:03,286
The held-out loss:

583
00:32:03,286 --> 00:32:06,617
Adam 5.189, rank 32 4.894, rank 8 4.957.

584
00:32:06,617 --> 00:32:10,364
GaLore wins, but most of the gap, 67 percent,

585
00:32:10,364 --> 00:32:13,695
comes from GaLore's smaller steps for the matrices,

586
00:32:13,695 --> 00:32:17,442
which let the rest train at the full rate:

587
00:32:17,442 --> 00:32:20,773
Adam given the same step sizes reaches 4.991.

588
00:32:20,773 --> 00:32:26,437
The low-rank window adds the remaining improvement in this experiment.

589
00:32:26,691 --> 00:32:28,706
Compare LoRA, Part 27's tool.

590
00:32:28,706 --> 00:32:34,350
LoRA keeps the change to the weights at rank at most r for good.

591
00:32:34,350 --> 00:32:39,590
GaLore keeps each step at rank at most r, but the window moves.

592
00:32:39,590 --> 00:32:44,427
After 600 steps, the total change to one matrix has an effective

593
00:32:44,427 --> 00:32:48,862
rank of 25 with the rank-8 window, against 80 for Adam:

594
00:32:48,862 --> 00:32:52,893
so its total change is far richer than rank 8.

595
00:32:52,893 --> 00:32:55,714
That is GaLore's answer to LoRA's limit;

596
00:32:55,714 --> 00:33:00,552
Part 27 uses LoRA where a small change is all that's wanted.

597
00:33:02,290 --> 00:33:03,281
Chapter twelve.

598
00:33:03,281 --> 00:33:06,757
Word vectors, from counting.

599
00:33:06,757 --> 00:33:10,399
Part 1 began with counts of distinct letter symbols.

600
00:33:10,399 --> 00:33:14,447
Now we connect words by counting the contexts they share.

601
00:33:14,447 --> 00:33:16,470
The idea comes from linguistics.

602
00:33:16,470 --> 00:33:19,708
In 1954 Zellig Harris argued that words in

603
00:33:19,708 --> 00:33:22,946
the same contexts tend to mean similar things.

604
00:33:22,946 --> 00:33:24,565
In 1957 John Firth:

605
00:33:24,565 --> 00:33:28,613
you shall know a word by the company it keeps.

606
00:33:28,613 --> 00:33:31,041
In 1990 Scott Deerwester, Susan Dumais

607
00:33:31,041 --> 00:33:33,470
and colleagues made it an algorithm:

608
00:33:33,470 --> 00:33:36,303
count words in documents, take the SVD,

609
00:33:36,303 --> 00:33:40,636
and search by meaning rather than spelling.

610
00:33:40,890 --> 00:33:43,865
We'll do it with the thirty novels.

611
00:33:43,865 --> 00:33:47,266
Take the ten thousand commonest words and count

612
00:33:47,266 --> 00:33:51,091
how often each pair turns up within four words:

613
00:33:51,091 --> 00:33:55,342
a hundred million cells, from 4.35 million words of text.

614
00:33:55,342 --> 00:33:58,742
Then turn counts into surprise, Part 1's way:

615
00:33:58,742 --> 00:34:03,418
the log of how many times more often two words meet

616
00:34:03,418 --> 00:34:06,393
than by chance, the pointwise mutual information.

617
00:34:06,393 --> 00:34:08,094
Keep its positive part.

618
00:34:08,094 --> 00:34:09,369
Monday and morning:

619
00:34:09,369 --> 00:34:09,794
4.16.

620
00:34:09,794 --> 00:34:14,044
The and of meet 85,087 times, but they meet everything:

621
00:34:14,044 --> 00:34:14,469
0.69.

622
00:34:14,469 --> 00:34:18,295
For the SVD table, we smooth the context distribution

623
00:34:18,295 --> 00:34:23,195
with the three-quarters power, then keep positive values.

624
00:34:23,490 --> 00:34:27,700
Keep the table's hundred strongest directions, with the SVD.

625
00:34:27,700 --> 00:34:30,507
Every word gets a hundred numbers.

626
00:34:30,507 --> 00:34:34,718
Words that keep the same company end up close.

627
00:34:34,718 --> 00:34:35,186
Monday:

628
00:34:35,186 --> 00:34:36,589
saturday, tuesday, sunday.

629
00:34:36,589 --> 00:34:37,057
Three:

630
00:34:37,057 --> 00:34:38,460
four, two, five.

631
00:34:38,460 --> 00:34:38,928
Dog:

632
00:34:38,928 --> 00:34:40,332
devil, dogs, perezvon.

633
00:34:40,332 --> 00:34:40,800
Ship:

634
00:34:40,800 --> 00:34:42,203
pequod, boat, sail.

635
00:34:42,203 --> 00:34:45,946
Perezvon is a dog in The Brothers Karamazov,

636
00:34:45,946 --> 00:34:48,753
and the Pequod is Ahab's ship.

637
00:34:48,753 --> 00:34:52,592
Nobody gave it a dictionary.

638
00:34:52,890 --> 00:34:55,401
How many directions does meaning need?

639
00:34:55,401 --> 00:34:57,493
Keep only the strongest few.

640
00:34:57,493 --> 00:35:02,096
With 2, only 0 of these twenty neighbours are final ones.

641
00:35:02,096 --> 00:35:05,025
With 5, 10, 25, and all 100.

642
00:35:05,025 --> 00:35:10,046
Our threshold is three of five neighbours among the final top ten.

643
00:35:10,046 --> 00:35:13,394
three first crosses it at 5 tested directions;

644
00:35:13,394 --> 00:35:14,649
monday at 100.

645
00:35:14,649 --> 00:35:17,997
This is a first crossing, not a claim

646
00:35:17,997 --> 00:35:20,507
that every later neighbour stays fixed.

647
00:35:20,507 --> 00:35:23,437
The strongest directions carry the broad patterns;

648
00:35:23,437 --> 00:35:27,029
finer meanings live further down.

649
00:35:27,290 --> 00:35:28,988
Directions mean something too.

650
00:35:28,988 --> 00:35:34,082
Step from France to Paris, then take the same step from England.

651
00:35:34,082 --> 00:35:37,902
The nearest word to where you land is London.

652
00:35:37,902 --> 00:35:41,298
Man is to king as woman is to:

653
00:35:41,298 --> 00:35:42,572
duke, then queen.

654
00:35:42,572 --> 00:35:45,968
On 710 such questions built from the books,

655
00:35:45,968 --> 00:35:49,788
man and woman, dog and dogs, walk and walked,

656
00:35:49,788 --> 00:35:54,033
the nearest word is right 41 percent of the time,

657
00:35:54,033 --> 00:35:56,155
out of ten thousand candidates.

658
00:35:56,155 --> 00:35:58,702
Separately, searching only among categorized words,

659
00:35:58,702 --> 00:36:01,674
of each category word's five nearest neighbours,

660
00:36:01,674 --> 00:36:04,221
animals, colours, numbers and so on,

661
00:36:04,221 --> 00:36:08,466
87 percent share its category, against 14 percent by chance.

662
00:36:10,223 --> 00:36:11,100
Chapter thirteen.

663
00:36:11,100 --> 00:36:16,547
Word2vec, which turns out to be counting in disguise.

664
00:36:16,557 --> 00:36:19,784
In 2013 Tomas Mikolov and colleagues at Google

665
00:36:19,784 --> 00:36:22,608
learned word vectors with a guessing game.

666
00:36:22,608 --> 00:36:28,257
Each word gets two vectors, one as the word and one as a neighbour.

667
00:36:28,257 --> 00:36:32,291
Training raises a word's dot product with its real neighbours,

668
00:36:32,291 --> 00:36:36,729
through the logistic function, and lowers it with 5 random words.

669
00:36:36,729 --> 00:36:40,764
That is skip-gram with negative sampling, the heart of word2vec:

670
00:36:40,764 --> 00:36:45,492
cheap enough for billions of words in hours.

671
00:36:45,757 --> 00:36:49,632
We trained it on the novels, with the same

672
00:36:49,632 --> 00:36:52,646
vocabulary and nominal window as the counting.

673
00:36:52,646 --> 00:36:56,091
Subsampling frequent words changes the actual context sequences.

674
00:36:56,091 --> 00:37:00,397
Four words and their nearest neighbours, with the loss below.

675
00:37:00,397 --> 00:37:02,120
At the start, random.

676
00:37:02,120 --> 00:37:05,565
After 36 steps, the lists are still mixed;

677
00:37:05,565 --> 00:37:07,718
some favour common function words.

678
00:37:07,718 --> 00:37:09,871
By step 1,089, meaning appears:

679
00:37:09,871 --> 00:37:12,454
three sits with four, six, two.

680
00:37:12,454 --> 00:37:17,191
By the end, Monday sits with Saturday, and father with mother.

681
00:37:17,191 --> 00:37:22,997
Nothing told it what a day or a relative is.

682
00:37:23,290 --> 00:37:25,715
Why do guessing and counting agree?

683
00:37:25,715 --> 00:37:29,354
In 2014 Omer Levy and Yoav Goldberg showed why.

684
00:37:29,354 --> 00:37:33,397
Collect the log objective for one word and one context:

685
00:37:33,397 --> 00:37:37,844
the pair appears as a real neighbour some number of times,

686
00:37:37,844 --> 00:37:41,482
and as a negative about k times the word's

687
00:37:41,482 --> 00:37:43,908
count times the context's sampling probability.

688
00:37:43,908 --> 00:37:46,737
Treat their dot product x as free;

689
00:37:46,737 --> 00:37:48,759
set the derivative to zero.

690
00:37:48,759 --> 00:37:53,610
The ratio of the two logistic terms is e to the x.

691
00:37:53,610 --> 00:37:54,419
Take logs:

692
00:37:54,419 --> 00:37:56,440
this maximizes the log objective.

693
00:37:56,440 --> 00:37:59,270
If negatives follow the empirical context distribution,

694
00:37:59,270 --> 00:38:02,908
the best dot product is PMI minus log k.

695
00:38:02,908 --> 00:38:05,738
With smoothed negatives, as in our run,

696
00:38:05,738 --> 00:38:09,377
use their sampling probability in the same log-count ratio:

697
00:38:09,377 --> 00:38:12,494
a generalized shifted PMI.

698
00:38:12,757 --> 00:38:16,179
Let's check it on the 300 commonest words,

699
00:38:16,179 --> 00:38:18,318
after three thousand Adam steps.

700
00:38:18,318 --> 00:38:22,596
We measure 82,030 observed pairs seen at least five times.

701
00:38:22,596 --> 00:38:28,157
With 300 numbers per vector, all dot products can be free in principle,

702
00:38:28,157 --> 00:38:31,580
but finite training is not a convergence certificate:

703
00:38:31,580 --> 00:38:35,858
the measured median error from generalized shifted PMI is 0.001.

704
00:38:35,858 --> 00:38:37,997
With 30, the points spread:

705
00:38:37,997 --> 00:38:38,425
0.18.

706
00:38:38,425 --> 00:38:39,708
With 5, 0.22.

707
00:38:39,708 --> 00:38:45,914
A short vector holds only a low-rank version of the table.

708
00:38:46,157 --> 00:38:51,248
And the real run, ten thousand words with a hundred numbers each?

709
00:38:51,248 --> 00:38:55,491
For the 6,288 pairs it saw at least 50 times:

710
00:38:55,491 --> 00:39:00,582
trained dot product against the shifted PMI of its own training pairs.

711
00:39:00,582 --> 00:39:02,280
Correlation 0.96, slope 0.97.

712
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:05,250
They agree closely, though the low rank

713
00:39:05,250 --> 00:39:09,295
pulls the extremes toward the middle.

714
00:39:09,557 --> 00:39:10,820
Which is better?

715
00:39:10,820 --> 00:39:15,451
On our analogy questions, counting gets 41 percent and word2vec 27.

716
00:39:15,451 --> 00:39:18,398
On analogies the counting method does better.

717
00:39:18,398 --> 00:39:20,924
On category neighbours, 87 against 79.

718
00:39:20,924 --> 00:39:24,292
Levy, Goldberg and Dagan found in 2015 that,

719
00:39:24,292 --> 00:39:28,081
tuned with equal care, the two families are close.

720
00:39:28,081 --> 00:39:31,449
GloVe, in 2014, fitted the log counts directly.

721
00:39:31,449 --> 00:39:34,817
All of these give one vector per word:

722
00:39:34,817 --> 00:39:39,027
bank by a river and bank with money share one.

723
00:39:39,027 --> 00:39:42,816
Parts 13 and 14 make vectors depend on context.

724
00:39:44,556 --> 00:39:45,407
Chapter fourteen.

725
00:39:45,407 --> 00:39:49,889
Life in seven hundred and sixty-eight dimensions.

726
00:39:49,889 --> 00:39:55,239
Our intuition comes from three dimensions, and in 768 it misleads us.

727
00:39:55,239 --> 00:39:56,577
Two random directions:

728
00:39:56,577 --> 00:39:58,806
in two dimensions, any angle.

729
00:39:58,806 --> 00:40:02,818
In three, right angles are already the most common.

730
00:40:02,818 --> 00:40:05,939
In ten, the spread is 18.8 degrees.

731
00:40:05,939 --> 00:40:09,506
In a hundred, the angle's standard deviation around

732
00:40:09,506 --> 00:40:15,019
ninety degrees is 5.8 degrees, and in 768, 2.1.

733
00:40:15,289 --> 00:40:15,713
Why?

734
00:40:15,713 --> 00:40:19,531
The cosine is the dot product over the lengths,

735
00:40:19,531 --> 00:40:22,925
each close to root d, as we'll see.

736
00:40:22,925 --> 00:40:26,743
The dot product is a sum of d independent

737
00:40:26,743 --> 00:40:29,712
products of mean zero and variance one:

738
00:40:29,712 --> 00:40:30,561
variance d.

739
00:40:30,561 --> 00:40:32,258
Divide by d squared:

740
00:40:32,258 --> 00:40:35,227
the cosine's variance is one over d.

741
00:40:35,227 --> 00:40:40,742
Measured from d of 2 to 4096, the points sit on the line;

742
00:40:40,742 --> 00:40:44,363
at 768, 0.00128 against 0.00130.

743
00:40:44,622 --> 00:40:46,690
So high-dimensional space is roomy.

744
00:40:46,690 --> 00:40:49,586
Ten thousand random directions in 768 dimensions:

745
00:40:49,586 --> 00:40:52,895
every pair between 78 and 102 degrees apart.

746
00:40:52,895 --> 00:40:57,031
A model stores an idea, a feature, as a direction,

747
00:40:57,031 --> 00:41:00,341
and can keep far more features than dimensions

748
00:41:00,341 --> 00:41:03,236
if they need only be nearly perpendicular:

749
00:41:03,236 --> 00:41:07,631
superposition, how Part 30 reads a model.

750
00:41:07,889 --> 00:41:08,717
Now lengths.

751
00:41:08,717 --> 00:41:13,687
As d grows, a random vector's length piles up around root d.

752
00:41:13,687 --> 00:41:19,898
Its square is a sum of d squares, with mean d and variance 2 d.

753
00:41:19,898 --> 00:41:24,868
The square root changes by one over two root d per unit,

754
00:41:24,868 --> 00:41:30,251
so the length's spread is about one over root two for large d:

755
00:41:30,251 --> 00:41:34,393
the absolute length grows, its absolute spread approaches a constant.

756
00:41:34,393 --> 00:41:39,362
The histogram divides length by root d, so its relative spread narrows.

757
00:41:39,362 --> 00:41:42,519
Measured at 768, 0.702.

758
00:41:42,756 --> 00:41:44,061
Volume is stranger.

759
00:41:44,061 --> 00:41:49,719
It grows like the radius to the d, so the inner ball with

760
00:41:49,719 --> 00:41:55,376
99 percent of the radius holds 0.99 to the d of the volume.

761
00:41:55,376 --> 00:41:59,728
In three dimensions, the outer one percent holds 3.0 percent.

762
00:41:59,728 --> 00:42:01,469
In a hundred, 63.4.

763
00:42:01,469 --> 00:42:03,209
In 768, 99.96 percent:

764
00:42:03,209 --> 00:42:06,256
a high-dimensional ball is nearly all skin.

765
00:42:08,022 --> 00:42:08,873
Chapter fifteen.

766
00:42:08,873 --> 00:42:13,355
How few numbers can keep the distances?

767
00:42:13,355 --> 00:42:15,546
A consequence that sounds impossible.

768
00:42:15,546 --> 00:42:19,051
Multiply a thousand of GPT-2's vectors by one

769
00:42:19,051 --> 00:42:22,556
random matrix, crushing 768 numbers to a few.

770
00:42:22,556 --> 00:42:25,185
These are 2,000 sampled ordinary-distance pairs,

771
00:42:25,185 --> 00:42:27,814
with one map per target dimension.

772
00:42:27,814 --> 00:42:30,443
Down to 2, distances are scrambled;

773
00:42:30,443 --> 00:42:31,320
5, 20:

774
00:42:31,320 --> 00:42:31,758
better.

775
00:42:31,758 --> 00:42:37,454
At 100, 84 percent of the distances move by less than ten percent;

776
00:42:37,454 --> 00:42:40,707
at 400, 99.6 percent.

777
00:42:40,955 --> 00:42:43,057
The Johnson-Lindenstrauss lemma, from 1984,

778
00:42:43,057 --> 00:42:45,999
in Dasgupta and Gupta's form of 2003.

779
00:42:45,999 --> 00:42:48,942
To keep every squared distance among n

780
00:42:48,942 --> 00:42:51,885
points within one plus or minus epsilon,

781
00:42:51,885 --> 00:42:55,668
a random projection to about log n over epsilon

782
00:42:55,668 --> 00:42:58,610
squared dimensions, up to a constant, suffices.

783
00:42:58,610 --> 00:43:01,553
For our thousand points at ten percent:

784
00:43:01,553 --> 00:43:01,973
5,921.

785
00:43:01,973 --> 00:43:04,916
The starting dimension doesn't appear at all.

786
00:43:04,916 --> 00:43:07,677
Let's prove it.

787
00:43:07,955 --> 00:43:12,933
Fix one pair, and let x be their difference, of length one.

788
00:43:12,933 --> 00:43:17,497
Each coordinate of R x is a sum of independent normals:

789
00:43:17,497 --> 00:43:19,156
normal, with variance one.

790
00:43:19,156 --> 00:43:23,305
The squared length is a sum of k such squares:

791
00:43:23,305 --> 00:43:24,549
chi-squared, mean k.

792
00:43:24,549 --> 00:43:25,794
The Chernoff trick:

793
00:43:25,794 --> 00:43:31,187
Markov's inequality on e to the t times it, whose average we know.

794
00:43:31,187 --> 00:43:34,921
The best t, and a bound on the logarithm:

795
00:43:34,921 --> 00:43:37,825
the chance of stretching past epsilon falls

796
00:43:37,825 --> 00:43:40,314
exponentially in k, and shrinking likewise.

797
00:43:40,314 --> 00:43:42,803
The union bound over all pairs:

798
00:43:42,803 --> 00:43:46,952
once k reaches four log n over that small quantity,

799
00:43:46,952 --> 00:43:50,271
the chance of any failure is below one.

800
00:43:50,271 --> 00:43:53,590
A good projection exists, and a random one

801
00:43:53,590 --> 00:43:58,409
works with probability at least one over n.

802
00:43:58,688 --> 00:43:59,961
Now the data.

803
00:43:59,961 --> 00:44:04,203
For one pair, the chance of moving more than 10

804
00:44:04,203 --> 00:44:07,597
percent is exactly a chi-squared tail, in yellow.

805
00:44:07,597 --> 00:44:09,294
These are squared-distance errors.

806
00:44:09,294 --> 00:44:12,263
The mean measured fraction over five maps

807
00:44:12,263 --> 00:44:15,657
differs from that probability by at most 0.0020;

808
00:44:15,657 --> 00:44:17,778
the bars show the range.

809
00:44:17,778 --> 00:44:20,323
Pairs sharing a map are dependent.

810
00:44:20,323 --> 00:44:24,141
The dashed Chernoff bound lies above the exact probability:

811
00:44:24,141 --> 00:44:27,762
at 768, 0.33 against 0.050.

812
00:44:28,022 --> 00:44:30,891
The lemma promises every pair at once.

813
00:44:30,891 --> 00:44:34,991
The worst pair of all, five random matrices per k.

814
00:44:34,991 --> 00:44:39,091
Bars show the minimum to maximum, with a mean dot.

815
00:44:39,091 --> 00:44:42,371
At 95 percent confidence over the random map,

816
00:44:42,371 --> 00:44:46,470
the first plotted nontrivial bound is at k of 400.

817
00:44:46,470 --> 00:44:50,160
At 768, it certifies squared-distance distortion at most 0.34;

818
00:44:50,160 --> 00:44:52,620
the measured worst-pair mean is 0.25.

819
00:44:52,620 --> 00:44:55,080
This confidence comparison is separate from

820
00:44:55,080 --> 00:44:57,540
the classic existence statement we proved.

821
00:44:57,540 --> 00:45:01,639
In the worst case no map can do much better:

822
00:45:01,639 --> 00:45:05,329
in 2017 Kasper Green Larsen and Jelani Nelson proved

823
00:45:05,329 --> 00:45:09,429
log n over epsilon squared optimal, up to a constant.

824
00:45:11,220 --> 00:45:12,161
Chapter sixteen.

825
00:45:12,161 --> 00:45:15,072
GPT-2's lopsided space.

826
00:45:15,087 --> 00:45:18,464
Random directions in 768 dimensions are nearly perpendicular,

827
00:45:18,464 --> 00:45:21,420
cosines of zero give or take 0.036.

828
00:45:21,420 --> 00:45:23,531
Trained vectors are not random.

829
00:45:23,531 --> 00:45:28,175
We sample two thousand token IDs from one thousand through nineteen

830
00:45:28,175 --> 00:45:31,552
thousand nine hundred ninety-nine, not the whole vocabulary.

831
00:45:31,552 --> 00:45:36,197
Between two of those token vectors the average cosine is 0.24:

832
00:45:36,197 --> 00:45:39,996
they all lean one way, the space is anisotropic.

833
00:45:39,996 --> 00:45:45,485
Each makes a cosine of 0.49, on average, with the full vocabulary mean.

834
00:45:45,485 --> 00:45:51,207
Subtract that mean, and the average cosine falls to 0.010.

835
00:45:51,487 --> 00:45:54,019
Even centred, the spread is uneven.

836
00:45:54,019 --> 00:45:57,817
The top principal direction carries 1.8 percent of it,

837
00:45:57,817 --> 00:46:00,349
against 0.13 for an even share.

838
00:46:00,349 --> 00:46:04,147
Colour each token by its number in GPT-2's vocabulary,

839
00:46:04,147 --> 00:46:08,367
a rough merge-order proxy rather than a measured frequency rank:

840
00:46:08,367 --> 00:46:12,587
the first direction tracks it, a rank correlation of 0.62.

841
00:46:12,587 --> 00:46:15,963
Jun Gao and colleagues explained this in 2019:

842
00:46:15,963 --> 00:46:19,339
training mostly pushes a rare token's vector away

843
00:46:19,339 --> 00:46:22,293
from contexts, so rare tokens drift together.

844
00:46:22,293 --> 00:46:24,403
Removing the top direction too,

845
00:46:24,403 --> 00:46:26,935
Mu and Viswanath's all-but-the-top of 2018,

846
00:46:26,935 --> 00:46:31,811
brings the average cosine from 0.010 to 0.0009.

847
00:46:32,087 --> 00:46:34,578
Inside the network it is stronger.

848
00:46:34,578 --> 00:46:37,899
GPT-2 reading the scoreboard's passage, layer by layer:

849
00:46:37,899 --> 00:46:42,051
the average cosine between positions is 0.13 at the input,

850
00:46:42,051 --> 00:46:46,203
about 0.38 in the middle, and 0.94 after final normalization.

851
00:46:46,203 --> 00:46:50,355
The maximum among non-input layers is 0.94 at layer 12.

852
00:46:50,355 --> 00:46:52,431
This illustrates the narrow-cone effect

853
00:46:52,431 --> 00:46:54,506
Kawin Ethayarajh measured in 2019.

854
00:46:54,506 --> 00:46:59,904
In layer 6, three of 768 dimensions carry 64 percent of the average

855
00:46:59,904 --> 00:47:04,471
cosine as a signed contribution, not a share of squared variance:

856
00:47:04,471 --> 00:47:07,792
rogue dimensions, in Timkey and van Schijndel's phrase.

857
00:47:07,792 --> 00:47:11,944
Centre each layer and the cosines fall to about zero.

858
00:47:11,944 --> 00:47:17,596
Raw cosines measure this common lean as much as meaning.

859
00:47:17,887 --> 00:47:20,350
Lopsided or not, directions carry meaning:

860
00:47:20,350 --> 00:47:23,223
our counted vectors sent England to london.

861
00:47:23,223 --> 00:47:26,096
In 2013 Mikolov, Yih and Zweig found

862
00:47:26,096 --> 00:47:28,559
such regularities in neural word vectors.

863
00:47:28,559 --> 00:47:32,664
In 2022 Nelson Elhage and colleagues showed toy networks storing

864
00:47:32,664 --> 00:47:35,948
more features than dimensions, as nearly perpendicular directions.

865
00:47:35,948 --> 00:47:41,285
In 2024 Kiho Park, Yo Joong Choe and Victor Veitch made it precise:

866
00:47:41,285 --> 00:47:44,569
concepts as directions, under the right inner product,

867
00:47:44,569 --> 00:47:47,032
not always the plain dot product.

868
00:47:47,032 --> 00:47:51,137
Reading and steering along such directions is Part 30's subject.

869
00:47:52,886 --> 00:47:53,777
Chapter seventeen.

870
00:47:53,777 --> 00:47:56,004
Low rank in the wild:

871
00:47:56,004 --> 00:47:58,820
recommendations and retrieval.

872
00:47:58,820 --> 00:48:03,266
Low rank's most famous test came from a DVD rental company.

873
00:48:03,266 --> 00:48:06,904
In October 2006 Netflix released a hundred million ratings,

874
00:48:06,904 --> 00:48:11,351
from 480,189 customers on 17,770 films, and offered a million dollars

875
00:48:11,351 --> 00:48:15,797
to predict held-out ratings ten percent better than its own system.

876
00:48:15,797 --> 00:48:17,818
The table was mostly empty:

877
00:48:17,818 --> 00:48:20,244
about two hundred ratings per customer.

878
00:48:20,244 --> 00:48:25,094
In December, a programmer writing as Simon Funk posted a short program

879
00:48:25,094 --> 00:48:29,541
that factorized it by gradient descent, and leapt up the leaderboard.

880
00:48:29,541 --> 00:48:33,987
In 2009 BellKor's Pragmatic Chaos won with a blend of hundreds

881
00:48:33,987 --> 00:48:39,125
of models, many of them refinements of that factorization.

882
00:48:39,420 --> 00:48:41,530
The model is a sum.

883
00:48:41,530 --> 00:48:45,750
An average, plus the user's generosity, plus the film's quality,

884
00:48:45,750 --> 00:48:49,970
plus a dot product of k numbers of taste each.

885
00:48:49,970 --> 00:48:54,612
Fit it to the ratings we have, keeping the vectors small,

886
00:48:54,612 --> 00:48:58,832
with a separate fixed penalty on user and film biases.

887
00:48:58,832 --> 00:49:00,942
Chapter eight solves the unregularized

888
00:49:00,942 --> 00:49:03,896
least-squares rank-k approximation of a full matrix.

889
00:49:03,896 --> 00:49:06,428
Here, we optimize the observed ratings

890
00:49:06,428 --> 00:49:08,960
with the vector and bias penalties.

891
00:49:08,960 --> 00:49:12,336
We built a small world to test on:

892
00:49:12,336 --> 00:49:14,868
4,000 users, 1,200 films, 122,937 ratings,

893
00:49:14,868 --> 00:49:19,744
from six hidden genres the method never sees.

894
00:49:20,020 --> 00:49:22,162
Training replayed, at rank 8.

895
00:49:22,162 --> 00:49:27,732
The error falls on the ratings it trains on, and on held-out ones:

896
00:49:27,732 --> 00:49:29,874
from 0.94 stars to 0.745.

897
00:49:29,874 --> 00:49:32,445
With no vector penalty, held-out error

898
00:49:32,445 --> 00:49:35,872
rises from its minimum of 0.831 to 0.858.

899
00:49:35,872 --> 00:49:40,157
Its final error is higher than the vector-penalized run's 0.745.

900
00:49:40,157 --> 00:49:43,156
Both runs retain the same bias penalty.

901
00:49:43,156 --> 00:49:47,869
On the right, each film's vector, on its two strongest directions,

902
00:49:47,869 --> 00:49:51,725
coloured by the hidden genre, with each genre's average.

903
00:49:51,725 --> 00:49:54,296
Of each film's ten nearest films,

904
00:49:54,296 --> 00:49:58,580
60 percent share its genre, against 19 percent by chance.

905
00:49:58,580 --> 00:50:00,294
The final genre-neighbour agreement

906
00:50:00,294 --> 00:50:03,936
exceeds the without-self chance baseline.

907
00:50:04,220 --> 00:50:06,788
How much taste does it take?

908
00:50:06,788 --> 00:50:11,497
All methods are refitted on the same training plus validation ratings,

909
00:50:11,497 --> 00:50:14,921
then measured on the same untouched test ratings.

910
00:50:14,921 --> 00:50:17,918
The average rating misses by 0.936 stars.

911
00:50:17,918 --> 00:50:19,630
Adding generosity and quality:

912
00:50:19,630 --> 00:50:20,058
0.822.

913
00:50:20,058 --> 00:50:24,767
Then the taste vectors, rank one to thirty-two, three seeds each.

914
00:50:24,767 --> 00:50:28,191
The error is lowest at rank 8, 0.746,

915
00:50:28,191 --> 00:50:33,544
near the six hidden genres in this synthetic world.

916
00:50:33,820 --> 00:50:35,488
Factorization has a weakness:

917
00:50:35,488 --> 00:50:37,989
a new user has no vector.

918
00:50:37,989 --> 00:50:40,074
Today's recommenders use two towers.

919
00:50:40,074 --> 00:50:42,993
One network turns a user's history into

920
00:50:42,993 --> 00:50:45,495
a vector, another a film's details;

921
00:50:45,495 --> 00:50:47,997
the score is their dot product.

922
00:50:47,997 --> 00:50:51,750
Trained so each user's real films outscore the batch,

923
00:50:51,750 --> 00:50:54,252
corrected for the negative sampling distribution.

924
00:50:54,252 --> 00:50:56,337
In the ideal candidate-classification calculation,

925
00:50:56,337 --> 00:51:00,923
the corrected logit carries a density ratio, while the raw score

926
00:51:00,923 --> 00:51:05,093
models the conditional log probability, up to a user constant.

927
00:51:05,093 --> 00:51:07,595
We rank by the raw score;

928
00:51:07,595 --> 00:51:10,514
adding a popularity prior again is a

929
00:51:10,514 --> 00:51:13,016
separate ablation, not a recovery step.

930
00:51:13,016 --> 00:51:15,935
Finite training need not reach that ideal.

931
00:51:15,935 --> 00:51:19,271
Macro recall averages each user's fraction of all

932
00:51:19,271 --> 00:51:22,606
eligible held-out favourites recovered in the top ten.

933
00:51:22,606 --> 00:51:25,525
New users start from five films each.

934
00:51:25,525 --> 00:51:28,444
For known users, macro recall reaches 0.097,

935
00:51:28,444 --> 00:51:32,614
against popularity's 0.081, on 600 users and 2961 eligible targets.

936
00:51:32,614 --> 00:51:35,950
The two towers beat popularity for this cohort.

937
00:51:35,950 --> 00:51:39,286
Separately, For new users, macro recall reaches 0.066,

938
00:51:39,286 --> 00:51:43,455
against popularity's 0.075, on 377 users and 7635 eligible targets.

939
00:51:43,455 --> 00:51:49,125
The two towers do not beat popularity for this cohort.

940
00:51:49,420 --> 00:51:52,277
Retrieval is, at heart, one matrix product,

941
00:51:52,277 --> 00:51:55,952
and the art is not computing most of it.

942
00:51:55,952 --> 00:51:58,809
In 2016 YouTube described recommending as a

943
00:51:58,809 --> 00:52:00,851
nearest-neighbour search among video vectors.

944
00:52:00,851 --> 00:52:04,933
In 2017 Facebook's FAISS searched a billion vectors, on GPUs.

945
00:52:04,933 --> 00:52:09,832
In 2020 dense passage retrieval put questions and passages in one space,

946
00:52:09,832 --> 00:52:13,915
the way assistants now find what to read before answering.

947
00:52:13,915 --> 00:52:16,364
Part 29 puts it to work.

948
00:52:18,153 --> 00:52:19,401
Let's take stock.

949
00:52:19,401 --> 00:52:22,315
Vectors are lists of numbers with geometry;

950
00:52:22,315 --> 00:52:26,477
the dot product, kept honest by Cauchy-Schwarz, measures likeness.

951
00:52:26,477 --> 00:52:29,806
Einsum names the axes and counts the cost.

952
00:52:29,806 --> 00:52:33,552
Projections find the closest point, and arithmetic's order matters.

953
00:52:33,552 --> 00:52:35,633
Symmetric matrices stretch along perpendicular

954
00:52:35,633 --> 00:52:37,714
directions, which power iteration finds;

955
00:52:37,714 --> 00:52:40,211
every matrix is turn, stretch, turn,

956
00:52:40,211 --> 00:52:43,957
and its biggest pieces are its best low-rank copy,

957
00:52:43,957 --> 00:52:46,871
with reflections allowed and rectangular singular-value blocks.

958
00:52:46,871 --> 00:52:50,617
Some trained weights and gradients concentrate in strong directions.

959
00:52:50,617 --> 00:52:53,946
Counting and guessing connect through related cooccurrence statistics;

960
00:52:53,946 --> 00:52:57,276
smoothed negative sampling gives a generalized shifted PMI.

961
00:52:57,276 --> 00:52:59,773
And in high dimensions, independent random

962
00:52:59,773 --> 00:53:01,438
directions are nearly perpendicular.

963
00:53:01,438 --> 00:53:04,351
Random projections can preserve squared distances with

964
00:53:04,351 --> 00:53:09,597
stated probability, and low rank helps fill empty tables.

965
00:53:09,886 --> 00:53:11,921
All of it comes back.

966
00:53:11,921 --> 00:53:15,992
Part 4 builds the chain rule that computes every gradient.

967
00:53:15,992 --> 00:53:19,248
Part 9 keeps each layer's stretch near one,

968
00:53:19,248 --> 00:53:22,097
so that deep networks train at all.

969
00:53:22,097 --> 00:53:26,167
Part 11 builds Muon, steepest descent in the spectral norm.

970
00:53:26,167 --> 00:53:29,831
Part 13 writes attention as dot products, in einsum.

971
00:53:29,831 --> 00:53:33,901
Part 15 squeezes a long context's memory through low rank,

972
00:53:33,901 --> 00:53:37,157
Part 27 fine-tunes through it, and Part 30

973
00:53:37,157 --> 00:53:41,914
reads and steers the directions inside a model.

974
00:53:42,153 --> 00:53:45,903
Here are eight problems to try, from the triangle

975
00:53:45,903 --> 00:53:47,986
inequality to the Johnson-Lindenstrauss proof.

976
00:53:47,986 --> 00:53:50,486
Pause here, and work through them;

977
00:53:50,486 --> 00:53:54,903
full solutions are in this part's notes.

978
00:54:00,886 --> 00:54:01,722
Next time:

979
00:54:01,722 --> 00:54:02,140
change.

980
00:54:02,140 --> 00:54:04,648
Everything in this part stood still,

981
00:54:04,648 --> 00:54:07,574
except in the chapters that trained something,

982
00:54:07,574 --> 00:54:10,082
where we took gradients on trust.

983
00:54:10,082 --> 00:54:14,262
Part 4 builds derivatives, the chain rule, and the machine

984
00:54:14,262 --> 00:54:19,942
that runs it backwards through billions of numbers at once.

985
00:54:20,219 --> 00:54:23,035
For more, Gilbert Strang's Linear Algebra and

986
00:54:23,035 --> 00:54:25,851
Learning from Data is a friendly companion,

987
00:54:25,851 --> 00:54:29,874
Trefethen and Bau explain why the order of arithmetic matters,

988
00:54:29,874 --> 00:54:31,886
and Roman Vershynin's High-Dimensional Probability

989
00:54:31,886 --> 00:54:35,506
explains why big spaces behave the way they do.

990
00:54:35,506 --> 00:54:39,127
Thanks for watching, and see you in Part 4.

